WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.302
These assessment tasks bring the main ideas together.

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:09.327
One task asks for sales revenue and unique products by category with a group threshold.

00:00:09.327 --> 00:00:16.317
Another asks for Fall twenty twenty-four course averages but only for courses with enough students.

00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:20.672
A third asks for separate diagnosis counts by patient.

00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:27.212
The final task asks for employee detail with department and company averages.

00:00:27.212 --> 00:00:30.972
Before writing any query, identify the grain and the type of calculation.

00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:38.852
Ask whether you need grouped rows or detail rows, whether the filter is on source rows or completed

00:00:38.852 --> 00:00:42.662
groups, and whether conditional aggregation or a window function fits the requirement.

00:00:42.662 --> 00:00:50.857
The coding exercises that follow break these ideas into smaller pieces so you can practice them independently.

00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:55.357
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:55.357 --> 00:01:00.632
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:01:00.632 --> 00:01:06.447
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:06.447 --> 00:01:10.587
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:17.647
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:17.647 --> 00:01:21.817
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:26.525
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
